def amazon(query,label):
 u='https://www.amazon.com/s?'+urlencode({'k':query,'tag':associate_tag})
 return '<a class="button" href="'+html.escape(u,quote=True)+'" target="_blank" rel="sponsored noopener noreferrer">'+label+' on Amazon (paid link) ↗</a>'
protein_body='''<article><a href="/">← All guides</a><p class="eyebrow">Protein / Product comparison</p><h1>Optimum Nutrition vs Orgain protein powder</h1><p class="lead">Start with dairy versus plant protein. Then compare equal amounts of protein, rather than scoop count.</p><p>By FitGear Authority editorial · Label sources checked November 1, 2026</p><div class="note">As an Amazon Associate I earn from qualifying purchases. This is a comparison of manufacturer information, not a hands-on taste or performance test. Check the exact flavor and size before ordering.</div><h2>Quick comparison</h2><div class="table-scroll"><table><caption>Two different protein formats</caption><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard 100% Whey</th><th>Orgain Organic Plant Based Protein</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Protein format</th><td>Whey; isolate is the primary source</td><td>Plant-based; described by Orgain as vegan</td></tr><tr><th>Listed protein per serving</th><td>24 g</td><td>21 g</td></tr><tr><th>First decision</th><td>Are dairy ingredients acceptable?</td><td>Do you want a plant-based product?</td></tr><tr><th>Check before buying</th><td>Exact flavor, allergens, serving count</td><td>Exact flavor, ingredients, serving count</td></tr><tr><th>Our taste test</th><td>Not performed</td><td>Not performed</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2>Which one fits your shopping needs?</h2><p>If you want to compare a whey powder and do not exclude dairy, start with the Gold Standard label. If you want a plant-based powder, start with Orgain. This is a choice of format, not a claim that one is universally better. Neither should be chosen solely because its tub has more scoops or a familiar brand name.</p><h3>Gold Standard 100% Whey: what to check</h3><p>The manufacturer lists 24 g protein per serving and identifies whey isolate as the primary protein source. That description does not mean the product is an isolate-only powder. Read the complete ingredient and allergen panels for the flavor you intend to buy.</p>'''+amazon('Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard 100% Whey protein powder','Find Gold Standard Whey')+'''<h3>Orgain Organic Plant Based Protein: what to check</h3><p>Orgain lists 21 g plant-based protein per serving. Its current Creamy Chocolate Fudge page describes a vegan formula. Confirm the exact product line: similarly named Orgain powders can have different ingredients. A plant-based label also does not replace checking your specific allergens.</p>'''+amazon('Orgain Organic Plant Based Protein Powder Creamy Chocolate Fudge','Find Orgain Plant Protein')+'''<h2>Compare value fairly</h2><p>A scoop is a manufacturer-defined measure. Comparing the price of one scoop from each tub can be misleading when protein quantities differ. Instead, record the delivered price, servings per package, and protein grams per serving for each exact listing. Use our <a href="/#calculator">protein value calculator</a> to compare cost for 25 g protein.</p><p>For an arithmetic example, a hypothetical $40 package with 30 servings and 24 g protein per serving costs about $1.39 per 25 g protein. That is a calculation example, not a current product price or a recommended serving. Include shipping and read subscription terms before comparing the total.</p><h2>When to skip either purchase</h2><p>Skip a large tub when you have not decided which flavor or format you will use. Skip a subscription if you only want a trial purchase. If the ingredient or allergen label is unclear, ask the manufacturer before ordering. This comparison cannot determine personal medical suitability.</p><h2>Questions before checkout</h2><details><summary>Does 24 g versus 21 g make one automatically better?</summary><p>No. The listed grams help compare quantities, but your ingredient preferences, full label, package cost, and intended use also matter.</p></details><details><summary>Have you tested taste or mixing?</summary><p>No. We have not tested these products. Manufacturer facts and our shopping interpretation are kept separate.</p></details><details><summary>Are these links to an exact Amazon listing?</summary><p>They open product-name searches. Match the exact formula, flavor, size, seller, and current label before purchasing.</p></details><h2>Sources and method</h2><p>Manufacturer sources: <a href="https://www.optimumnutrition.com/en-us/products/gold-standard-100-whey-protein-powder">Optimum Nutrition product page</a> and <a href="https://orgain.com/products/organic-protein-plant-based-protein-powder">Orgain product page</a>. These establish label information, not independent performance testing. Our value method and shopping tradeoffs are editorial analysis.</p><p>Keep comparing: <a href="/guides/whey-protein.html">whey guide</a> · <a href="/guides/plant-protein.html">plant protein guide</a> · <a href="/guides/budget-protein.html">budget comparison checklist</a>.</p></article>'''
creatine_body='''<article><a href="/">← All guides</a><p class="eyebrow">Creatine / Product comparison</p><h1>Nutricost vs Optimum Nutrition creatine</h1><p class="lead">Compare unflavored micronized creatine monohydrate powders using the ingredient panel and cost per gram.</p><p>By FitGear Authority editorial · Manufacturer sources checked November 1, 2026</p><div class="note">As an Amazon Associate I earn from qualifying purchases. We have not tested these powders. This page compares shopping details, not medical suitability or guaranteed results.</div><h2>Quick comparison</h2><div class="table-scroll"><table><caption>What the manufacturer pages describe</caption><thead><tr><th>Shopping detail</th><th>Nutricost Creatine Monohydrate Powder</th><th>Optimum Nutrition Micronized Creatine Powder</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Named form</th><td>Creatine monohydrate</td><td>Creatine monohydrate</td></tr><tr><th>Powder description</th><td>Micronized</td><td>Micronized</td></tr><tr><th>Variant to compare</th><td>Unflavored; match package size</td><td>Unflavored; match package size</td></tr><tr><th>Useful value unit</th><td>Delivered cost per gram of creatine</td><td>Delivered cost per gram of creatine</td></tr><tr><th>Independent head-to-head test here</th><td>None</td><td>None</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2>Choose the listing before choosing the price</h2><p>Both manufacturer pages identify micronized creatine monohydrate. That shared description is a starting point, not evidence that the finished products perform identically or that one is superior. Compare the exact unflavored variants if you want a straightforward comparison; flavored products may add ingredients and change the amount of powder per serving.</p><h3>Nutricost: check size and flavor together</h3><p>Nutricost’s product page offers multiple flavors and sizes. Confirm both selection controls and the matching supplement facts panel. A large bottle or a low headline price can belong to a different variant than the one you intended to compare.</p>'''+amazon('Nutricost Creatine Monohydrate Powder Unflavored','Find Nutricost Creatine')+'''<h3>Optimum Nutrition: check the powder format</h3><p>Optimum Nutrition sells a Micronized Creatine Powder. Check that a listing is for the powder you intend to compare, rather than capsules, gummies, or a blend. Use the listed amount of creatine, not package appearance, to calculate value.</p>'''+amazon('Optimum Nutrition Micronized Creatine Powder Unflavored','Find Optimum Nutrition Creatine')+'''<h2>How to calculate value</h2><p>Divide the delivered price by the total grams of the named creatine ingredient. For single-ingredient products, the net weight can help; for blends or flavored formulas, use the amount of creatine per serving multiplied by servings. Do not assume all powder weight is creatine.</p><p>As a hypothetical shopping example, $30 for 500 g of creatine is $0.06 per gram; $24 for 300 g is $0.08 per gram. The lower bottle price is not necessarily the lower unit price. These are arithmetic examples, not current merchant prices, product offers, or dosing advice.</p><h2>How to make the decision</h2><ol><li>Match the named form and exact variant.</li><li>Check the full ingredient and allergen panels.</li><li>Verify any certification claim in the certifier’s directory for that exact product.</li><li>Compare delivered cost for equal creatine quantities.</li><li>Check seller identity and return terms before checkout.</li></ol><p>If those checks do not distinguish the options, price and practical package size may be more useful than marketing language. We do not have independent laboratory results or taste tests that justify declaring a winner.</p><h2>Common questions</h2><details><summary>Does micronized mean a different ingredient?</summary><p>In these product names, the manufacturers still identify creatine monohydrate. Read the full panel instead of treating the word micronized as a separate ingredient or a guarantee of better results.</p></details><details><summary>Can I assume either product is certified?</summary><p>No. This comparison does not verify certification. Use the <a href="/guides/third-party-testing.html">certification checklist</a> for the exact product and variant.</p></details><h2>Sources and method</h2><p>Manufacturer sources: <a href="https://nutricost.com/products/nutricost-creatine-monohydrate-powder-500-grams">Nutricost product page</a> and <a href="https://www.optimumnutrition.com/en-us/products/creatine-monohydrate-micronized-powder">Optimum Nutrition product page</a>. Product-format facts come from these pages. Shopping examples and selection criteria are our editorial analysis.</p><p>Read next: <a href="/guides/creatine.html">creatine label checklist</a> · <a href="/guides/beginner-checklist.html">first supplement checklist</a>.</p></article>'''
